@article{Salop1982,
   abstract = {or "underbites", seen in OI patients have typically been attributed to shortening of the maxilla relative to the mandible, 7-9 but have alternatively been attributed to mandibular prognathism. Dentinogenesis imperfecta (DI) is also commonly co-occurrent with OI type III and typically affects the deciduous teeth more severely than the permanent dentition. 4,8 Reduced body size and altered body proportions are a hallmark of OI type III, 10,11 yet few studies of the craniofacial morphology associated with the disorder have taken this into consideration. In both experimental and clinical literature, absolute craniometric distances have often used to assess phenotype differences between affected and unaffected populations despite known differences in overall body size. 12,13 An exception to this trend is the clinical classification of macrocephaly. O'Connell and Marini 4 found that absolute or "true" macrocephaly characterized only some children with types III and IV OI, but relative macrocephaly (head circumference scaled to body height) was present in the majority of these patients. More recently, Xu et al 14 reported decreased nasal length, increased neurocranial length and width, and increased basicranial length when these measurements were normalized to skull length in the Crtap −/− mouse model of OI type VII. These findings underscore the significance of size for our ability to identify altered phenotypes, particularly in affected groups where size is so dramatically altered from the population norm. Understanding the differences in craniofacial shape (ie, the proportional morphological differences removed from the effects of overall skull size) has the potential to aid in the identification of those regions of the skeleton that are disproportionately affected by OI. The identification of specific craniofacial regions with altered growth will help us to understand the processes by which these complex craniofacial phenotypes arise, and to identify behavioral and/or pharmaceutical interventions to assist in the recovery of the cranio-facial phenotype. A large body of recent work has employed mouse models of OI in order to understand the basis of the disease and potential avenues for therapeutic treatment. There are several current mouse models of OI 15,16 or combined OI and Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome (EDS) 13 used in research. These models report postcranial phenotypes similar to those seen in human patients with OI, with a limited number of studies also reporting human-like alterations to cranial and dental integrity. 1,13,14 In this study, we investigate size and shape changes in the craniofacial skeleton of the homozygous osteogenesis imperfecta murine (OIM −/−), a model for the severe OI type III. OIM −/− mice have decreased incisor mineralization compared to homozygous and wild-type (WT) littermates, 1 but little else is known about the craniofacial phenotype in this murine model. We test two specific hypotheses 1 : that the OIM −/− mouse exhibits the small body and cranial sizes seen in human patients with OI type III; and 2 that these global changes in body size underlie the localized changes in cranio-facial shape reported in both humans and mice with genetic defects of type I collagen. 2 | MATERIALS AND METHODS 2.1 | Experimental model All procedures for this study were conducted in accordance with an Indiana University School of Medicine (IUSM) Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) approved protocol. The osteogenesis imperfecta murine (OIM) is a mouse strain with a nonlethal recessive inherited mutation of the COL1A2 gene. Homozygous OIM −/− mice (B6C3FE a/a-Co-l1a2OIM/J) are a model for the human presentation of OI type III. The wild-type (WT) control in this experiment are OIM +/+ (B6C3FeF1/J) mice. All animals were bred and housed in an AAALAC-accredited vivarium. WT and OIM −/− littermates were weaned and genotyped at 21 days, and raised until early adulthood (16 weeks old). This endpoint was chosen since skeletal maturity, defined as the cessation of growth of gross skeletal dimensions, has been observed in laboratory mice at 60 days (8.5 weeks) for the cranium and between 4 and 6 months (16-24 weeks) for the postcranial skeleton. 17,18 Previous experimental studies reporting the craniofacial phenotypes for other murine models of OI have sampled from a range of ages [eg, 8 weeks 14 ; 4 and 20 weeks 13 ], which may represent a potential limitation for the direct comparison of phenotypes among the strains. Body weights were collected twice a week, and regular visual inspections of the dentition were performed to ensure that no malocclusions developed. Where incipient malocclu-sions of the incisors were noted, veterinary staff performed minimal trimming so as to ensure normal feeding. No molar malocclusions were noted during the course of the experiment. At the end of the experimental period, all animals were euthanized via CO 2 inhalation. Bilateral pneumothorax was used as a secondary means of euthanasia. 2.2 | MicroCT Imaging and 3D landmark digitization All animals were imaged in vivo using micro-computed to-mography (micro-CT) at 16 weeks of age. Imaging was performed by SkyScan 1176 microCT unit (Bruker Corp, MA, USA) with reconstructions using 0.008 or 0.017 mm 3 vox-els, dependent upon cranial length. During imaging, animals},
   author = {S Salop and J.E. Stiglitz},
   issue = {5},
   journal = {American Economic Review},
   pages = {1121-1130},
   title = {The Theory of Sales: A Simple Model of Equilibrium Price Dispersion with Identical Agents},
   volume = {75},
   url = {https://www.jstor.org/stable/1812027},
   year = {1982}
}
